1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:07,458
The princess Sāvitrī shone so brightly that no prince dared ask for her hand.

2
00:00:07,458 --> 00:00:10,258
So she went forth to choose for herself —

3
00:00:10,258 --> 00:00:15,516
and in a forest hermitage she chose Satyavān, son of a blind and exiled king.

4
00:00:17,716 --> 00:00:22,940
But the sage Nārada told her father: this youth is flawless in every virtue —

5
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:25,654
and one year from this day, he will die.

6
00:00:25,654 --> 00:00:30,267
Sāvitrī answered only: "The heart is given once. I have given mine."

7
00:00:32,467 --> 00:00:36,923
She put away her silks, wore bark-cloth, and served the hermitage —

8
00:00:36,923 --> 00:00:39,184
telling no one, counting the days.

9
00:00:39,184 --> 00:00:44,304
For the last three nights she stood fasting, still as a lamp in windless air.

10
00:00:46,504 --> 00:00:50,497
On the appointed morning she followed him into the forest.

11
00:00:50,497 --> 00:00:56,073
At noon, the axe slipped from Satyavān's hand. "My head," he said, "is splitting.

12
00:00:56,073 --> 00:00:59,033
" And he laid it in her lap, and was still.

13
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:11,726
Then Sāvitrī saw a figure dark as a rain-cloud, robed in red, a noose in his hand: Yama, King of the Dead, come in person for so virtuous a soul.

14
00:01:11,726 --> 00:01:16,979
He drew out a spark the size of a thumb — and turned south. And she followed.

15
00:01:19,579 --> 00:01:24,197
Walk with the wise but seven steps, she said, and you are friends.

16
00:01:24,197 --> 00:01:28,605
Pleased, Yama offered boons — anything but the life he carried.

17
00:01:28,605 --> 00:01:31,754
Sight for the blind king. A kingdom restored.

18
00:01:31,754 --> 00:01:37,841
And then: "a hundred sons, born of me and Satyavān." The Lord of Death stopped walking.

19
00:01:40,641 --> 00:01:44,693
For her word could not come true unless his life was returned.

20
00:01:44,693 --> 00:01:48,549
Caught by his own boon, Yama smiled — and loosed the noose.

21
00:01:48,549 --> 00:01:57,699
Satyavān woke as if from sleep; his father's eyes opened on the world; and Sāvitrī walked her husband home at dusk, having out-argued Death.
